<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A STRICT SUBSET of the PADneXt 2.12 ADL payload schema. NOT the official XSD.

  The official schema (padx_adl_v2.12.xsd, PADline GmbH / PVS-Verband, published at
  padinfo.de) is not redistributable here the way the GOÄ text is — the GOÄ is an amtliches
  Werk and public domain under § 5 UrhG, a trade association's interface specification is
  not. So this file is ours: hand-written, licensed with the repository, and describing only
  what `app/padnext/reader.py` needs a delivery to be before it is worth auditing. An
  operator who holds the official schema can drop it in `data/licensed/padnext/` and the
  validator will prefer it — see `app/padnext/schema.py`.

  ============================================================================================
  WHAT THIS SCHEMA IS FOR, AND THE ONE RULE THAT SHAPES ALL OF IT
  ============================================================================================

  This engine audits invoices. The invoices most likely to be WRONG are the malformed ones, so
  a validator that refuses anything imperfect would refuse exactly the files a practice most
  needs read. Therefore:

      FRAMING is fatal.       Is this an ADL payload at all? Wrong root, wrong namespace, no
                              message type, a counter that is not a number, a treatment code
                              outside the legal set — these mean the document cannot be
                              audited, or cannot be priced, so the delivery is refused.

      POSITIONS are advisory. A `goziffer` missing its `@ziffer`, a `faktor` reading "zwei",
                              an `auslagen` line this engine does not model — these are found,
                              reported per position, and priced as far as they can be. That is
                              the product: `app/padnext/audit.py` gives every claimed line a
                              verdict and every verdict a reason.

  Everything below follows from that split, which is why this schema is deliberately CLOSED at
  the top of the document and deliberately OPEN inside an invoice.

  ============================================================================================
  DELIBERATE DIVERGENCES FROM THE OFFICIAL SCHEMA — each one is a decision, not an omission
  ============================================================================================

  1. `goziffer/@ziffer` is OPTIONAL here; the official schema requires it. A position with no
     Ziffer is reported as `padnext_position_without_ziffer` and excluded from the money.
     Rejecting the delivery instead would lose the other eight positions on the invoice.

  2. Every leaf carrying a NUMBER is `xs:string`, not `xs:decimal`/`xs:integer`: faktor,
     einzelbetrag, punktzahl, punktwert, gesamtbetrag, minderungssatz. The reader parses these
     into `Decimal` and reports what it cannot parse (`padnext_unparsable_number`). An
     unreadable amount on one line is a finding about that line, not grounds to refuse the
     invoice. Note the asymmetry with @anzahl/@posanzahl below: those are FRAMING counters the
     specification puts there for cross-checking, and a cross-check that silently does not run
     is worse than one that fails.

  3. `rechnung` and `abrechnungsfall` have OPEN content. A real delivery carries elements this
     engine does not model — patient identity, diagnoses, invoice-level totals — and a closed
     model would reject every real file. `processContents="lax"` means the children we DO
     declare globally (`abrechnungsfall`, `positionen`, `behandlungsart`) are still validated
     wherever they appear, so openness costs no strictness where strictness was the point.

  4. `positionen` has OPEN content, so `gozziffer`, `auslagen`, `entschaedigung`,
     `sonstigeshonorar` and `text` positions are accepted and reported as
     `padnext_position_type_not_modelled` rather than refused. Silently dropping them would
     understate an invoice without saying so.

  5. `goziffer` has OPEN content too, for the same reason: an unmodelled child element inside
     one position must not cost the whole delivery. Its known children and attributes are
     listed in the comment on that declaration for documentation, since XSD 1.0 cannot both
     enumerate them and stay open (`xs:all` admits no `xs:any`).

  6. `rechnungen` is the one CLOSED model, and that is the framing contract: after
     `nachrichtentyp` a payload may carry only `rechnungsersteller`, `leistungserbringer` and
     `rechnung`. A foreign element at the top of the document means this is not the message
     type it claims to be.

  7. There is no `minOccurs="1"` on `rechnung`, on `abrechnungsfall`, or on `positionen` — not
     because they are optional, but because the reader already refuses their absence with a
     message that names what to send instead (`read_delivery`, and the
     `padnext_abrechnungsfall_without_positionen` invariant in `app/padnext/schema.py`). XSD
     1.0 cannot express "this element is required, and unknown siblings are still welcome":
     `xs:any` next to a required particle is a non-deterministic content model and libxml2
     refuses to compile it. Given the choice between rejecting real deliveries and checking
     one invariant in code with a line number attached, the invariant wins.

  Structure, for orientation (`*` = repeatable, `?` = optional, "…" = open):

      rechnungen @anzahl
        nachrichtentyp @version
        rechnungsersteller ? | leistungserbringer * | rechnung *
          rechnung @id @aisrechnungsnr
            abrechnungsfall …
              behandlungsart ? | vertragsart ? | minderungssatz ? | …
              positionen @posanzahl
                goziffer @positionsnr @go @ziffer @analog …
                gozziffer | auslagen | entschaedigung | sonstigeshonorar | text …
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
           xmlns:pad="http://padinfo.de/ns/pad"
           targetNamespace="http://padinfo.de/ns/pad"
           elementFormDefault="qualified">

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       Simple types
       ========================================================================== -->

  <!--
    § 6a GOÄ pricing turns on this code, which is why it is the one enumerated value in this
    schema. The set is the specification's: 0 ambulant, 1 stationär, 2 teilstationär,
    3 vorstationär, 4 nachstationär, 5 Konsiliarbehandlung.

    3, 4 and 5 are ACCEPTED here and then reported as `padnext_behandlungsart_not_mapped` by
    `derive_setting`, because they do not map cleanly onto a § 6a reduction and guessing one
    would misprice a whole invoice. A value outside the set is different in kind: it is not a
    treatment setting the engine has an opinion about, it is not a treatment setting at all, and
    falling back to "ambulant" would apply a 0 % reduction to an invoice whose pricing basis is
    unknown. That is wrong money, so it is fatal.
  -->
  <xs:simpleType name="Behandlungsart">
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:enumeration value="0"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="1"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="2"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="3"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="4"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="5"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- An element whose content this engine does not model. Mixed, open, and open-attributed:
       it exists so a real delivery's extra structure passes through instead of being refused. -->
  <xs:complexType name="Opaque" mixed="true">
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:any namespace="##any" processContents="lax" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
    <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       Global declarations
       ========================================================================== -->

  <!--
    Declared globally on purpose. Everything below `rechnung` is validated with
    `processContents="lax"`, which validates a child only when a global declaration for its
    name exists. Making these four global is therefore what keeps the open content models
    strict about the things that matter: a `behandlungsart` is checked against the enumeration,
    and a `posanzahl` against its datatype, wherever in the document they turn up.
  -->

  <xs:element name="behandlungsart" type="pad:Behandlungsart"/>

  <!--
    A `goziffer` as this engine reads it. Known children, all optional, none type-constrained:

        datum, anzahl, text, zusatztext, faktor | einzelbetrag, begruendung,
        minderungssatz, punktzahl, punktwert, gesamtbetrag

    The content model is open rather than an `xs:all` over that list, so an unmodelled child
    inside one position does not cost the delivery — divergence 5 above. The attributes are
    named for documentation and are all `xs:string`, including `@ziffer` (divergence 1) and
    `@go`, which must stay free-form: a GOZ or BG position is reported `out_of_scope` by the
    audit, not refused here.
  -->
  <xs:element name="goziffer">
    <xs:complexType mixed="true">
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:any namespace="##any" processContents="lax" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      </xs:sequence>
      <xs:attribute name="positionsnr" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:attribute name="go" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:attribute name="ziffer" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:attribute name="analog" type="xs:string"/>
      <!-- `##any`, not `##other`: an unprefixed attribute belongs to no namespace at all, so
           `##other` would make `<goziffer sonderfeld="x">` fatal — a position-level surprise
           refusing the whole delivery, which is the one thing this schema must not do. -->
      <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>

  <!--
    `@posanzahl` is the specification's own consistency counter, and it is typed for the reason
    given in divergence 2: the reader compares it against the positions it actually read
    (`padnext_position_count_mismatch`). A value like "viele" does not fail that comparison — it
    silently cancels it, leaving an invoice that was never cross-checked looking like one that
    was.
  -->
  <xs:element name="positionen">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:any namespace="##targetNamespace" processContents="lax"
                minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      </xs:sequence>
      <xs:attribute name="posanzahl" type="xs:nonNegativeInteger"/>
      <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>

  <!-- Open content: a real Abrechnungsfall carries patient identity, diagnoses and dates that
       this engine deliberately never parses. `positionen` and `behandlungsart` are still
       validated inside it, being global. -->
  <xs:element name="abrechnungsfall">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:any namespace="##targetNamespace" processContents="lax"
                minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      </xs:sequence>
      <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       The document
       ========================================================================== -->

  <!--
    The framing contract, and the only closed model in this schema.

    `nachrichtentyp` is required and must come first: a payload that does not say what message
    type it is cannot be audited as an ADL delivery, and the reader would otherwise fall back to
    the order file's claim about a document that never made one.

    After it, a repeating choice rather than a sequence — `rechnungsersteller`,
    `leistungserbringer` and `rechnung` in any order and any number. A choice of named elements
    is a deterministic content model, which a sequence with a trailing wildcard is not, and the
    order of those three is not something worth refusing a delivery over.

    `@anzahl` is typed for the same reason as `@posanzahl`.
  -->
  <xs:element name="rechnungen">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element name="nachrichtentyp">
          <xs:complexType>
            <xs:simpleContent>
              <xs:extension base="xs:string">
                <xs:attribute name="version" type="xs:string"/>
              </xs:extension>
            </xs:simpleContent>
          </xs:complexType>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:choice minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
          <xs:element name="rechnungsersteller" type="pad:Opaque"/>
          <xs:element name="leistungserbringer" type="pad:Opaque"/>
          <xs:element name="rechnung">
            <xs:complexType>
              <xs:sequence>
                <xs:any namespace="##targetNamespace" processContents="lax"
                        minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
              </xs:sequence>
              <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
            </xs:complexType>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:choice>
      </xs:sequence>
      <xs:attribute name="anzahl" type="xs:nonNegativeInteger"/>
      <xs:anyAttribute namespace="##any" processContents="lax"/>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
</xs:schema>
